  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nRest of Today-Thursday...A mid-level disturbance located within\nthe subtropical jet looks to move through north Florida into this\nevening. This feature, combined with PWATs in excess of 1.5\", is\nforecast to produce scattered showers and storms south of Cape\nCanaveral over the next several hours. Isolated showers are\nbeginning to develop over this area as of around 2 PM. A few\nstorms could be strong to locally severe, with moderate deep-layer\nshear and 500 mb temperatures -10 to -11 C. The main threats will\nbe gusty winds 50-60 mph and coin-sized hail. Convection is\nforecast to move offshore through the evening hours (PoPs 20-40%),\nwith drier conditions by daybreak Thursday.\n\nAs high pressure washes out, a low over the northern US drifts\neastward. A weak, initial cold front associated with this feature is\nexpected to pass through east central Florida Thursday, helping to\nreduce rain chances. A low chance (20%) for showers and storms\nlingers along the southern Treasure Coast into the afternoon.\nOtherwise, PoPs remain below 10%. Westerly winds 10-15 mph could\nproduce wind gusts near 20 mph at times. While not noticeably \nless humid, the front will help to return high temperatures to \nnearer to normal (the upper 70s to low 80s). Also, low \ntemperatures fall into the upper 50s north of Orlando/Cape \nCanaveral Thursday night.\n\nFriday-Saturday...An upper level low strengthens over the Great\nLakes Friday into Saturday, deepening the surface feature over the\nNortheast. Locally, a stronger cold front is forecast to pass\nsouthward through the peninsula Friday morning and into the\nafternoon, ushering in a much drier, cooler continental airmass. A\nfew showers may accompany the frontal passage prior to sunrise\nFriday morning near and north of Orlando. Otherwise, PoPs remain\nbelow 10% through Saturday, as PWATs fall to 0.5\" or less.\n\nNorthwesterly winds 10-15 mph, with gusts to 20 mph, are expected\nFriday, helping to keep high temperatures in the lower to mid-70s.\nBy Saturday, continued northerly winds will lead to well below\nnormal high temperatures, only reaching the mid to upper 60s.\nFriday night looks to be the coolest night, before veering winds\nbring slightly warmer temperatures off of the Atlantic Saturday\nnight. Lows in the mid to upper 40s for all but the Space and\nTreasure Coasts, combined with winds 5-10 mph, will lead to wind\nchills in the lower to mid-40s in the coldest spots early\nSaturday. By early Sunday, lows in the 50s, with upper 40s only\nnorth and west of I-4, improve wind chills into the mid to upper\n40s over the interior.\n\nSunday-Early Next Week...Stout high pressure builds across the\neastern half of the US on Sunday, veering winds onshore. Moisture\nbegins to return to the area, though PoPs remain below 20%, and\ntemperatures warm to nearer to normal to close out the weekend.\nInto early next week, models are in fairly good agreement that an\nupper level wave looks to undercut ridging to the north of\nFlorida, pushing a disturbance eastward through the Gulf. However,\nthey differ on timing, available moisture, and the strength of\nthis feature. Will need to monitor the threat for locally heavy\nrainfall Monday into mid-week, due to lingering effects of last\nweek's extreme rainfall, but it is too early to determine where or\nwhen heavy rainfall may occur. For now, there is slowly increasing\nconfidence in a return of rain chances and an unsettled pattern \nnext week, especially along the coast.",
